"Grand Theft Auto VI" can theoretically now run on Apple silicon Macs via unofficial community projects, despite Rockstar Games offering no official Mac version.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/grand-theft-auto-iv-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1440" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1079785" /><br/>
Developers have adapted the Xbox Series version of the game to work on macOS unofficially, with some reporting that they can explore scenes and control the camera. Unfortunately, the projects remain experimental and performance is too slow for normal play.<br/>
<br/>
Mac owners can already play GTA VI through Remote Play from a compatible console. These unofficial projects instead run the game locally on the Mac itself.<br/>
<br/>
One project, "<a href="https://github.com/halfwave50/VIctory-macOS-halfwave50">Halfwave</a>," reports around 3 frames per second in a prison scene and almost 5 frames per second during a boat sequence on an M4 Pro <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> with 48GB of memory. A separate test with 1080p output reached 6 frames per second in the boat scene. The developer also says saved games and free roam work, although lighting errors, flickering objects, and slow-loading scenery remain.<br/>
<br/>
Halfwave builds on "<a href="https://github.com/pragyaangaur/VIctory-macOS">VIctory-macOS</a>," another community effort to run the game on Apple's chips. A <a href="https://github.com/pragyaangaur/VIctory-macOS/issues/50">tester using an M2 Pro</a> Mac reported reaching the opening movement tutorial and controlling the camera, but at approximately one frame every two seconds. <br/>
<br/>
The projects use several translation tools as a workaround to make the Xbox game work on a Mac. Rosetta 2 handles processor instructions, CrossOver Wine provides Windows compatibility, and Apple's D3DMetal translates graphics into a format the Mac can display. AI tools are also helping with the projects.<br/>
<br/>
Take-Two Interactive, Rockstar's parent company, is already targeting efforts to bring the game to other platforms unofficially. A <a href="https://github.com/github/dmca/blob/master/2026/11/2026-11-24-take-two.md">copyright complaint published by GitHub</a> alleged that a similar project called "Leonida" infringed Take-Two's rights.<br/>
<br/>
